    {
      "id": "scene_pkzmyvVtFo",
      "type": "slide",
      "order": 3,
      "title": "CSS Syntax and Basic Rules",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_QIFABzkX",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_kBbhqXFr"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_tKA6_59v",
          "text": "Alright, let’s dive into the building blocks of CSS: selectors, properties, and values.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_Fm3u69Uu",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_VCP2Y15x"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_jM2D3zDU",
          "text": "First, a selector tells the browser which HTML element you want to style, a property is the aspect you’re changing—like color or font size—and the value is the specific setting you assign, such as red or 16px.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_dQecRE1U",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "shape_Q-uKVUt6"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_-HjXBaqv",
          "text": "Visually, think of a rule‑set as a tiny instruction card: the selector is the address, the braces hold the instructions, and each line inside follows a property‑value pair.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_0k9N5Ncz",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_Janndh-y"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_nrig_5I4",
          "text": "Now, let’s write our first declaration together: <code>selector { color: red; }</code> – this tells the browser to make the text of the selected element appear red.",
          "type": "speech"
        }
      ],
