    {
      "id": "scene_wPRgBBTgHI",
      "type": "slide",
      "order": 5,
      "title": "Ways to Add CSS to a Page",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_VerdgcVu",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text__s5QiCwj"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_2oby3RdD",
          "text": "Alright, let's explore the three ways we can add CSS to a page.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_kIy9PKk6",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_Rs-ZhlzV"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_rgwIKKQ8",
          "text": "First, inline CSS—this lets you style a single element right inside its HTML tag, which is handy for quick, one‑off tweaks.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_uCr3eEg3",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_lFaVC_lM"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_cbCepmpf",
          "text": "For example, here we have a paragraph with a style attribute that sets the text color directly.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_9E1udhVN",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_Rs-ZhlzV"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_2T2JmEgq",
          "text": "Next, internal CSS—placed inside a <style> block in the head of the document, it lets you style multiple elements on the same page without touching each tag.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_t6FlZh1Z",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_Rs-ZhlzV"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_iEBwZZzN",
          "text": "Finally, external CSS—stored in its own .css file and linked to the HTML, which is the most scalable approach for larger sites.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_zFKcCDK6",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text__s5QiCwj"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_jA5zHNx3",
          "text": "In practice, we choose the method that matches how often we need to reuse styles and how maintainable we want our code to be.",
          "type": "speech"
        }
      ],
      "content": {
        "type": "slide",
        "canvas": {
          "id": "gcEmofhbx6NwgTml-0OX4",
          "theme": {
            "shadow": {
